new protests are planned over the police  killing of an unarmed black man in  Sacramento after prosecutors declined to  charge the officers 22 year old Stephon  Clark was shot and killed last month in  his grandmother's backyard now this case  led to nationwide protests Jamie yuccas  shows us how Clark's family is now  demanding justice  I would like accountability I want  justice and accountability  Steve on take Clark is calling on  California's Attorney General to  intervene after the Sacramento County  District Attorney decided not to charge  the two officers responsible for his  brother's death police fired multiple  shots at 22-year old Stefan Clark in his  grandmother's backyard last March while  responding to reports of vandalism the  coroner says he was hit seven times on  Saturday District Attorney Anne Marie  Schubert announced that the officers had  reason to believe their lives were at  risk  she said Clark seen here in a grainy  image ignored commands he moved toward  the officers who saw this flash of light  they thought was a gun authorities later  discovered it was a cell phone the  decision ignited protests outside the  police department over the weekend and  at the small what we're asking for  clearly is for those two killer police  officers to be fired immediately  investigators determined that Clark had  been smashing car windows while under  the influence of drugs the night of his  death they also revealed he was possibly  suicidal after a domestic violence  incident involving his fiancee still  Clark's family insists those details do  not justify his death Stefan is dreams  being fulfilled not just a kid who died  in his grandmother's backyard for CBS  this morning  Jamie yuccas Los Angeles no thanks to  Jamie
 

